@page "/learn/quickgrid-url-paging"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Grid state in the URL · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Grid state in the URL</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You sort a grid and go to page 2, then refresh. Why are you back on page 1?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Static SSR · about 4 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> QuickGrid reads its sort, direction and page from the URL, and renders its
        headers and pager as links. So it sorts and pages on a static page, and every view is a URL you can share.
        In .NET 10 the grid kept that state inside the component, and on a static page its headers and pager did
        nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a products grid on a static page: sortable columns and a pager, nothing else. It was asked for
        <code>?sort=Price&amp;direction=desc&amp;page=2</code>, the second page, highest price first.
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 11 changed QuickGrid itself, so the old grid can't run in this app. Fig. 1 replays what a real .NET 10
        build returned for that query string. It comes from a small repro with its own data: 25 placeholder
        products and two columns, so its rows won't match Fig. 2's Northwind products. Compare what each grid does
        with the URL, not the rows. The replay's buttons are as inert as they were.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A sortable grid on a static page, recorded on .NET 10" Url="demo/grid-url/products/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            The line inside the frame, <code>GET /static?sort=Price&amp;direction=desc&amp;page=2</code>, is the
            request the .NET 10 app was sent; the frame's own address is just where this app keeps the replay. The
            grid ignored the query: it shows page 1, sorted by Product. Click <b>Price</b>, or <b>›</b> under the
            grid. Nothing happens: no request, no error, no change. (<b>⟳</b> and <b>Start over</b> just reload the same replay.)
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The headers and pager are buttons with nothing behind them</h2>
    <p>Here's what .NET 10 sent for the Price header and the next-page arrow:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The markup .NET 10 returned</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;button class="col-title" type="button"&gt;Price&lt;/button&gt;   <span class="err">no handler</span>
&lt;button class="go-next" type="button"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;          <span class="err">no handler</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 10 the headers and pager are buttons with click handlers. On a static page there's no circuit to
        run a handler, so a click goes nowhere. The grid's sort and page live in the component, and it never looks
        at the URL. So a refresh, or a shared link, always starts from page 1.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Upgrade, and write the grid as usual</h2>
    <p>There's nothing to turn on. An ordinary QuickGrid and Paginator, on a static page, is the whole fix:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix: the grid you'd write anyway</span>
        <pre class="snip">@@page "/demo/grid-url/products"
<span class="dim">@@* no @@rendermode, so the page is static SSR *@@</span>

&lt;QuickGrid Items="products" Pagination="pagination"&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn <span class="hl">Title="Price"</span> Property="p =&gt; p.UnitPrice"
                    <span class="hl">Sortable="true"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;
&lt;Paginator State="pagination" /&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, QuickGrid reads <code>sort</code>, <code>direction</code> and <code>page</code> from the query
        string. It renders each sortable header and pager arrow as a link to the next view's URL. Every click is a
        plain request for a new URL, with no circuit. <code>sort=Price</code> finds the column whose
        <code>Title</code> is Price. Here's the same query string, on the .NET 11 grid.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same query string, on .NET 11" Url="demo/grid-url/products?sort=Price&direction=desc&page=2"
                Label=".NET 11 · live" Side="after">
        <Caption>
            The address carries Fig. 1's query string, and this time the grid obeys it: page 2, highest price
            first, as the header arrow and the pager say. Click <b>Price</b>, or
            <b>‹</b> under the grid, and watch the frame's address: the URL changes with the view. Press <b>⟳</b>
            to refresh, and the view stays.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/GridUrl/Products.razor" Feature="quickgrid-url-paging">
        <Caption>
            No render mode, and no paging or sorting code of its own. Fig. 1's repro used the same pattern,
            sortable <code>PropertyColumn</code>s plus a <code>Paginator</code> on a static page, with two columns
            instead of three, on QuickGrid 10.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Make it interactive, or hand-roll it</h2>
    <p>A .NET 10 build rendered the same grid two ways, and showed what each costs:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The same grid in .NET 10, and what it did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/static"</pre>
                <span class="why">headers and pager are buttons with no handler; sort and page in the URL are ignored</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ clicks do nothing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/interactive"
<span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">clicks work over a WebSocket circuit, but the URL never changes</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a refresh or shared link lands on page 1, sorted by Product</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Going interactive also costs a circuit, and server memory, for every visitor, just to sort a table. The
        other way out was to give up QuickGrid's sorting and pager: read <code>sort</code> and <code>page</code>
        with <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code>, and write your own header links and
        <code>OrderBy</code>, <code>Skip</code> and <code>Take</code> for every grid.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Every QuickGrid keeps its view in the URL</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 this is how QuickGrid works, with nothing to opt into. On a static page, headers and pager are
        links, and enhanced navigation fetches the next view without a full reload. With an interactive render
        mode, the grid keeps the URL in step as you click. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing to opt into also means your existing grids change when you upgrade. Their sortable headers render
        as <code>&lt;a class="col-title"&gt;</code> links instead of buttons, so CSS that targeted
        <code>button.col-title</code> stops matching. If that bites, an AppContext switch brings the buttons back;
        it's in Good to know below.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Sorting matches the column title.</b> Rename a column, and old links that sort by it fall back to the default sort.</li>
        <li><b>Two grids on one page?</b> Give each a prefix: <code>new QueryParameterNameOptions("cities_")</code> makes them <code>cities_sort</code>, <code>cities_direction</code> and <code>cities_page</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Turning it off.</b> The AppContext switch <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.QuickGrid.EnableUrlBasedQuickGridNavigationAndSorting</code> set to <code>false</code> brings the buttons back.</li>
        <li><b>Your own query parameters survive.</b> A filter read with <code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</code> stays in the grid's links.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
